# Cursor agent workflow (HARD RULES)

These rules override helpfulness. When in doubt, **stop and ask** — do not guess.

**No deferrals without the operator's word:**
[`no-silent-deferrals.mdc`](/.cursor/rules/no-silent-deferrals.mdc) — **HARD.** Finish the whole
ask. No "fold forward", no self-authored out-of-scope list, no MVP reported as done. Soft plan
wording is not a skip; only an explicit "defer X" / "skip X" from the operator defers a piece, and
you quote it.

## Sub-agent models

Locating and mapping code → the cheap default model and `cavecrew-investigator`, never the
expensive tier. The expensive tier is for hard analysis and for coding when delegated. Full table:
[`subagent-model-routing.mdc`](/.cursor/rules/subagent-model-routing.mdc).

## Who writes what

| Work | How it is done |
|---|---|
| Plan review, critique, tickets, specs, plans, documentation passes | in the Cursor chat (modes A and B below) |
| Application code for a ticket | through the ticket tooling — `cargo xtask ticket run`, or `cargo xtask platform slice-run <id>` for one ticket — with any AI coding agent, or in the chat when the operator says so (mode C) |
| Application code in the platform factory | slice agents the orchestrator dispatches into `slice/<id>` worktrees (mode F) |
| Documentation of changed code | the agent or person who changes the code, in the same commit ([commit checklist](/documentation_v2/standards/commit_checklist.md)) |

**Executor gate:** act on a ticket only when its `executor` is `claude-code` (any AI coding agent)
or `cursor-docs` (a ticket, spec or documentation pass). `workbench`, `human` or `ci` — **stop and
wait** for that party.

## Four modes — infer them from intent, not from magic phrases

The operator speaks loosely; that is fine. **Classify the intent** of the message — never require
exact wording.

### Mode A — Plan review (read-only)

**The message sounds like:**
- a pasted plan from another agent plus "what do you think", "is this right", "does this make
  sense";
- "look at this plan", "review this", "anything wrong here";
- "give me something to send back", "fix the plan first";
- a first message that is only a plan, with no "write it" / "ship it" / "file it".

**Allowed:** read-only exploration, critique, a list of what the plan misses, and **one copy-paste
prompt that asks the planning agent to revise its plan**.

**FORBIDDEN:** any file write, ticket change, application code, or "completing" plan todos.

**End with:** the revise-the-plan prompt as one fenced block (no code, no files).

**Prompts for other agents:** follow
[`claude-prompt-delivery.mdc`](/.cursor/rules/claude-prompt-delivery.mdc) — **one complete fenced
block per stream**, never a prose summary.

### Mode B — Ticket, spec and documentation pass

**The message sounds like:**
- "write the ticket", "set it up", "queue it", "make the handoff", "do the docs";
- "ok looks good", "approved", "yeah do that", "prep it for the coding agent";
- agreement after a mode A exchange, asking for the ticket, spec and plan;
- "document this", "same as we always do", "like the last ticket".

**Allowed:** ONE ticket by default, filed with `cargo xtask ticket add` and filled in the engine's
canonical form; its spec from [`spec_template.md`](/.ai/tickets/spec_template.md) and its plan
from [`plan_template.md`](/.ai/tickets/plan_template.md); the spec's copy-paste prompt block per
[`implementation_prompt.md`](/.ai/tickets/implementation_prompt.md); a handoff from
[`handoff_template.md`](/.ai/tickets/handoff_template.md) when the context is too long for the
spec; an audit report the operator approved; documentation under `documentation_v2/`. Then
`cargo xtask ticket check --strict`. The steps are
[Taking a ticket from idea to shipped](/documentation_v2/runbooks/ticket_run_pipeline.md) steps 1
to 5.

**FORBIDDEN:** application source edits. Many tickets at once, unless the operator asks.

**If A or B is unclear:** ask one line — *"Review only, or write the ticket and spec?"*

### Mode C — Code

**The message sounds like:**
- "fix it", "implement", "ship the hotfix", "run the ticket";
- "implement the plan" with no ticket written yet.

**Default response:** code runs through the ticket tooling. Say `cargo xtask ticket run` (after
`--dry-run`), or print the prompt with `cargo xtask ticket prompt <id>`. Do not patch application
sources (`apps/website/api_v2/src/`, `apps/website/frontend/src/`, `apps/mod/`) in this chat unless
the operator **clearly** says so: *"Cursor, you write the code"*. When the operator does, the code,
its tests and its documentation land together in one commit.

### Mode F — Platform factory

**This SUPERSEDES mode C for platform-factory work.** When the operator says "run the factory",
"dispatch wave N" or "continue the factory", or names a platform wave, the chat is the factory's
orchestrator: it dispatches slice agents into git worktrees, integrates, gates, runs an
adversarial verifier, triages and pushes — and never implements.

Read [`platform-factory-mode.mdc`](/.cursor/rules/platform-factory-mode.mdc), then
[Factory waves](/documentation_v2/runbooks/factory_waves/README.md). Do **not** infer factory mode
from a vague "implement this" — ask one line when it is not explicit.

## Danger patterns — still read carefully

| Vague message | Safe default |
|---|---|
| "Implement / execute / build / go ahead" | No ticket and spec yet → **mode A or B**, not code. Ask if one sentence is unclear. |
| "Don't stop until done" | Finish the work of the current mode; code for a ticket goes through the ticket tooling. |
| Long ramble ending in "…so yeah" | Re-read the whole message for a pasted plan (A), an approval (B), or a fix request (C). |

## Pre-flight (before ANY edit)

Before writing files, state the **mode (A/B/C/F)** and list **every path** you will touch.

If the list includes any of:
- `apps/website/api_v2/src/**`
- `apps/website/frontend/src/**`
- `apps/mod/**`

and the operator has not explicitly authorized code in this chat → **STOP**.

## Typical thread flow

```text
1. Operator → Cursor: a plan from another agent (often rambling — still mode A)
2. Cursor: critique, plus a revise-the-plan prompt (no files)
3. Operator → planning agent: sends the prompt
4. Operator → Cursor: the revised plan
5. Cursor: confirm or nitpick (still mode A until the operator wants it set up)
6. Operator: approval ("ok", "set it up", "write the ticket") → mode B
7. Cursor: ONE ticket + spec + plan (+ handoff when needed), marked ready; ticket check --strict
8. Operator: cargo xtask ticket run (after --dry-run), or the prompt from cargo xtask ticket prompt
9. The coding agent: code, tests and the documentation they change, in one commit
```

## Codebase audit and tech debt

- **Default: ONE ticket whose spec lists every finding, all fixed in one pass** — do not split it
  into sibling tickets unless the operator asks.
- Running beside another ticket is fine when the two file sets do not overlap.

## Commits

- Commit only when the operator asks.
- A commit carries the code and the documentation it changes together, on `main`, with the
  ticket id in the subject ([commit checklist](/documentation_v2/standards/commit_checklist.md)).

## When you mess up

If the operator says stop or undo: revert only the edits you made, delete only the untracked files
you created, and confirm the tree is back to where you found it. Another session's uncommitted
work stays untouched. Do not argue.
